    "reasoning": "All 16 mathematical answers agree with their own captured Math Academy worked solutions. q-19899: three distinct foods have 3!=6 orders. q-65990: tan(π/3)=√3 and tan(-π/4)=-1, with both angles in arctan's principal range; point I fails, so II and III only. q-70788: point I satisfies both the tangent equation and principal-range restriction; point II fails the equation, and point III lies outside the range. q-118117: excluding v=0,-4, clearing denominators gives 6v+12=3v+12 and hence the excluded value v=0, so there are no solutions. q-104469: -2x(x+5)(x-1)=-2x³-8x²+10x; the solution's repeated root subscript x₁ is a harmless authored indexing typo. q-107540: 2x(x+3)(x-5)=2x³-4x²-30x. q-90536: the principal arcsine is -π/4, so half is -π/8. q-117456: arctan(1)=45°, equivalently π/4 radians. q-7645: setting y=0 gives (x-2)²=20/9 and the two stated intercepts; the choice's ± notation represents both points. q-70184: setting y=0 gives (x+2)²=-72, which has no real solution. q-13762 and q-75474: left multiplication by the compatible identity matrix leaves each matrix unchanged. q-41949: multiplication by (1,0,0)ᵀ selects A's first column. q-11007: combining the selected fractions gives numerator (5x+3)(x-5)-4(x²+11)=x²-22x-59. q-11014: combining the selected fractions gives (3x+1)(x-1)+2(x²+2x+3)=5x²+2x+5. q-23642: using csc²θ=1+cot²θ reduces the expression to (1+cot²θ)²-cot⁴θ-2cot²θ=1 wherever the original expression is defined. Confirmation rests on captured worked solutions, not on inferred grading.",
